  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "When the side length of a square is increased by 3, its area is quadrupled. Find the side length of the original square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "3 units.",
      "worked": "Let $x>0$ be the original side length. The new side length is $x+3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+3)^2&=4x^2\\\\x^2+6x+9&=4x^2\\\\3x^2-6x-9&=0\\\\x^2-2x-3&=0\\\\(x-3)(x+1)&=0\\\\x&=3\\text{ or }-1\\end{align*}$$\nReject $x=-1$ because a side length is positive. The original side length is 3 units.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
